-- Rust's `ref` and `ref mut` pattern binders. UNREACHABLE: regraded 2026-11-10.
--
-- `docs/GRAMMAR.md` graded these `PASSTHROUGH` against `examples/destructuring.sdx`
-- and cited *"match ergonomics make these rare, and the spelling is Rust's"*.
-- Both halves were wrong. The spelling is not reachable at all, and that
-- example contains no `ref` — the row was graded reachable and witnessed by
-- nothing, which is the shape plan 51's `construct_coverage` exists to find
-- and the gap its `ALLOW` list recorded.
--
-- `ref` is **not a reserved word** (`crates/redox-lexer/src/token/keyword.rs`
-- lists none), so `ref a` lexes as two ordinary identifiers and the pattern
-- parser sees two names where it expects one: `RDX0101` and then `RDX0103`.
--
-- Reserving it is not free either. Doc 18 §7's reserved set is deliberately
-- small, and `ref` is a plausible *identifier* in Redox — a reference, a
-- bibliography entry, a git ref — so taking the word costs every program that
-- uses it as a name.
--
-- The nearest working thing is what the original note half-remembered: match
-- ergonomics. `case &pair of (a, b) ->` binds `a` and `b` by reference
-- without the keyword, which is how `examples/destructuring.sdx` actually
-- does it and why nobody noticed the gap. What has no spelling is the case
-- where ergonomics do not apply and a binder must be explicitly `ref mut`.

first_of : &(String, u32) -> &String
first_of p = case p of
  (ref a, _) -> a

main : ()
main =
  pair = (String::from "x", 1)
  print (first_of (&pair))
